<http://data.silknow.org/event/4ed3483c-15cd-330c-be50-9c59abe3c3c8>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This fragment of blue silk with a silver pattern may have been part of the last order for furnishing textiles made by the administrator of the Crown Furniture Repository (Gardemeuble) in France in 1791. He requested 300 ells (approximately 400 yards) of fabric for Queen Marie Antoinette's apartments at the palace of Saint Cloud. The order was delivered in 1792, just a few months before she was guillotined. It was called a 'blue and silver damask lampas' in the contemporary documents. The original design had been supplied by the Lyonnais designer Joseph Gaspard Picard (1750-1818) in 1791 and would have been approved or adapted according to the instructions of the Gardemeuble's designer Jean Démosthène Dugourc.\nThe entire quantity of the fabric was used by the Revolutionary government in 1796 to pay off a national debt to Greek merchants who may have considered melting down the silver to change it into currency. The silk was originally intended as both upholstery and as wallhangings. According to the original order, the pattern was composed of three subjects: firstly,  children, vases and cupids, secondly, animals at rest, such as lions, leopards or tigers, and thirdly, the departure of Diana (goddess of hunting) for the hunt. The border was to be made up of caryatides with a brownish ground. Camille Pernon, the most prominent of silk manufacturers in Lyon, undertook to manufacture the fabric which was to cost about 50 livres per ell. By the time of the order's delivery in April 1792, inflation had raised the cost to over 76 livres per ell.  The total was extremely extravagant given that at that time a master weaver in Lyon might earn about 1,800 livres per year and the lowliest employee in the silk industry earned only 200 livres per year. Similar designs in less costly materials (blue and white) were woven in the 19th and 20th century, and there are also wallpapers in similar colours with similar motifs.\n\nPicard was an orphan by the time he moved to Lyon at the age of 16 when he moved to Lyon. In Lyon he was pupil of Joseph Bournes, then a designer and partner in several silk houses, member of the Chamber of Commerce and the Académie de Lyon. He was apparently in the manufacturing partnership, Reboul (L.), Fontebrune, Beraye et Picard working out of rue Royale, maison Pitiot in 1788.  Reboul Fontebrune et cie had supplied the Royal Household with a ‘damas lampas 3 couleurs fond bleu, gris et blanc’ in an arabesque design in 1785-6 for the salon des Jeux du roi at Fontainebleau – 536 ells @ 36 livres per ell for wide fabrics and 144 elles @ 26 livres for smaller borders. It was subsequently used on different occasions in other rooms.The partnership also sent samples of ‘lampas fond vert lilas et argent, lampas idem broché argent, lampas idem bleu et argent’ which did not result in orders.  Both the Comte du Nord (heir to Russian throne) and Joseph II (of Austria) visited his workshop.The secretary of the Académie de Lyon, Jean Baptiste Dumas, read a eulogy before the Académie after Picard's death. He suggested that Picard's  father had destined him for a career as an avocat, but orphaned, he came to Lyon and became a designers. He was considered an equal of Lasalle, Bourne and Bony, the first rank of designers. He resisted invitations to emigrate to other silk manufacturing countries."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/031a7a21-6b14-3078-a569-1b90e05710b5> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This fragment of blue silk with a silver pattern may have been part of the last order for furnishing textiles made by the administrator of the Crown Furniture Repository (Gardemeuble) in France in 1791. He requested 300 ells (approximately 400 yards) of fabric for Queen Marie Antoinette's apartments at the palace of Saint Cloud. The order was delivered in 1792, just a few months before she was guillotined. It was called a 'blue and silver damask lampas' in the contemporary documents. The original design had been supplied by the Lyonnais designer Joseph Gaspard Picard (1750-1818) in 1791 and would have been approved or adapted according to the instructions of the Gardemeuble's designer Jean Démosthène Dugourc.\nThe entire quantity of the fabric was used by the Revolutionary government in 1796 to pay off a national debt to Greek merchants who may have considered melting down the silver to change it into currency. The silk was originally intended as both upholstery and as wallhangings. According to the original order, the pattern was composed of three subjects: firstly,  children, vases and cupids, secondly, animals at rest, such as lions, leopards or tigers, and thirdly, the departure of Diana (goddess of hunting) for the hunt. The border was to be made up of caryatides with a brownish ground. Camille Pernon, the most prominent of silk manufacturers in Lyon, undertook to manufacture the fabric which was to cost about 50 livres per ell. By the time of the order's delivery in April 1792, inflation had raised the cost to over 76 livres per ell.  The total was extremely extravagant given that at that time a master weaver in Lyon might earn about 1,800 livres per year and the lowliest employee in the silk industry earned only 200 livres per year. Similar designs in less costly materials (blue and white) were woven in the 19th and 20th century, and there are also wallpapers in similar colours with similar motifs.\n\nPicard was an orphan by the time he moved to Lyon at the age of 16 when he moved to Lyon. In Lyon he was pupil of Joseph Bournes, then a designer and partner in several silk houses, member of the Chamber of Commerce and the Académie de Lyon. He was apparently in the manufacturing partnership, Reboul (L.), Fontebrune, Beraye et Picard working out of rue Royale, maison Pitiot in 1788.  Reboul Fontebrune et cie had supplied the Royal Household with a ‘damas lampas 3 couleurs fond bleu, gris et blanc’ in an arabesque design in 1785-6 for the salon des Jeux du roi at Fontainebleau – 536 ells @ 36 livres per ell for wide fabrics and 144 elles @ 26 livres for smaller borders. It was subsequently used on different occasions in other rooms.The partnership also sent samples of ‘lampas fond vert lilas et argent, lampas idem broché argent, lampas idem bleu et argent’ which did not result in orders.  Both the Comte du Nord (heir to Russian throne) and Joseph II (of Austria) visited his workshop.The secretary of the Académie de Lyon, Jean Baptiste Dumas, read a eulogy before the Académie after Picard's death. He suggested that Picard's  father had destined him for a career as an avocat, but orphaned, he came to Lyon and became a designers. He was considered an equal of Lasalle, Bourne and Bony, the first rank of designers. He resisted invitations to emigrate to other silk manufacturing countries."@en .

